<p>Brave and DuckDuckGo Browser are practical Arc Search alternatives if your priority is cleaner mobile browsing with built-in protections. Firefox suits cross-device browsing and compatible Android extensions; Safari is worth trying first on an iPhone. Microsoft Edge is a candidate for AI assistance inside a mobile browser. Perplexity's mobile app is closer to the answer-search part of Browse for Me, but is only a partial browser replacement. Keep Arc Search if its quick searches and reading workflow still fit. Choose what you need to replace first: the browser, the AI answers, or the connection to desktop Arc.</p>
<h2>Arc Search is a mobile browser, not desktop Arc</h2>
<p><a href="https://arc.net/search">Arc Search</a> combines regular mobile browsing with Browse for Me, built-in blocking, reader mode, and inactive-tab archiving. The product is free to download. Its <a href="https://resources.arc.net/hc/en-us/articles/20887042551831-Arc-for-iOS-Android-Arc-Search">mobile documentation</a> links iOS and Android installations and describes Arc Sync. It also marks some pinning functions as iOS-only, so identical behavior across both phones should not be assumed.</p>
<p>Do not confuse it with the deprecated Arc Mobile Companion app. Nor should an alternative article about Arc Search automatically become a list of desktop browsers that imitate Arc's sidebar. This comparison focuses on what happens when you search, read, save, and open links on a phone.</p>
<p>There is a development-status qualification. <a href="https://arc.net/">Arc's desktop homepage</a> says Arc receives Chromium updates only and directs readers to Dia for active security patches. That desktop notice does not prove that every mobile feature has been shut down. The <a href="https://resources.arc.net/hc/en-us/articles/27306904379031-Arc-Search-for-Android-Release-Notes">Android release notes</a> include August 7, 2026 URL-spoofing fixes and Android compatibility updates. The <a href="https://apps.apple.com/in/app/arc-search-find-it-faster/id6472513080">iOS store listing</a> also records recent bug and security improvements. Download availability and fixes are evidence of availability, not a guaranteed future feature roadmap.</p>
<h2>When changing one setting is enough</h2>
<p>Keep Arc Search if its compact interface, reading flow, and tab cleanup are why you use it. If your only objection is the default AI search, investigate a setting before migrating. Arc's <a href="https://resources.arc.net/hc/en-us/articles/25482594475031-How-To-Set-a-Default-Search-Engine-in-Arc-Search">search-engine guide</a> describes changing Browse for Me to another listed engine, including conventional search options.</p>
<p>Consider switching when you need a particular mobile extension, a different desktop browser ecosystem, stronger control over saved material, or an AI answer app with a different research workflow. These are specific requirements. We do not infer widespread failures from individual store reviews or claim that any listed tool is more accurate on every query.</p>
<h2>A shortlist by the job it replaces</h2>
<p>The comparison uses official product, support, store, and pricing pages checked October 8, 2026. It is research-based. We did not measure search accuracy, browsing speed, battery use, or blocking performance. The examples are suggested evaluation tasks, not reported testing outcomes.</p>
